fact WorkItem(id: "WI-778", description: some(value: "An ENTITY field or OPERATION parameter with a missing name or type is SILENTLY accepted, interning the empty string as a field name. Tree-sitter recovers the absent token as a ZERO-WIDTH node which is neither ERROR nor MISSING, so Node::has_error() is false, collect_syntax_errors (rustland/anthill-core/src/parse/mod.rs, the '!has_error() && !is_missing()' prune) never descends, and the converter's 'self.intern(self.text(n))' runs on empty text with no guard. MEASURED SILENT (parses clean, zero diagnostics): 'entity e(: Int64)', 'entity e(a: )', 'operation f(: Int64) -> Int64'. The resulting field label is real, not dropped -- under the tuple-type instance of the same bug the empty label reached a type mismatch rendered as 'got (: Int64)'. This directly violates CLAUDE.md's 'prefer a loud error over a silent skip'. SCOPE, measured: the TUPLE TYPE instance of this class is already FIXED -- WI-766 unified the arrow parameter list and tuple_type into one production, and '(: Int64)', '(a: )', '(a: Int64,)' and '(Int64,)' are all loud parse errors now (the last two were silently recovering a PHANTOM zero-width component into a 2-tuple before, on both the pre-WI-766 grammar and the rejected conflict-based variant). What remains is the entity-constructor and operation-parameter walkers, which are separate code paths and were never touched. Same pathology WI-440 fixed for '@ {}' -- see the _effect_set comment in grammar.js describing 'error-recovered into a zero-width simple_type ... with the annotation silently dropped'. PREFER a producer-side guard (a loud check on empty field name/type text in parse/convert.rs) over per-consumer checks, so every walker inherits it; note there are at least three walkers (entity constructor, operation params, arrow params). ACCEPTANCE: each measured-silent case above is a loud located error naming the missing part; a regression test covers entity and operation sites and asserts the MESSAGE (is_err alone would pass on any syntax error); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T06:42:33Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-778", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED at 971a5aed, with two corrections to this ticket. (1) THE DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG: this ticket states Node::has_error() is false so the '!has_error() && !is_missing()' prune never descends. Measured, has_error() is TRUE on the zero-width node and on every ancestor -- the walk always reached it and dropped it at the BOTTOM, in the 'interior node that merely contains an error -- descend' arm, which iterated its zero children and vanished. The prune is innocent. (2) FIXED AT THE WALK, NOT the producer-side guard in parse/convert.rs this ticket prefers: one arm in collect_syntax_errors keyed on byte_range().is_empty() closes the hole before conversion, so all ~31 intern(text(n)) sites inherit it. Also: this ticket lists THREE producers, but only TWO are in this class -- an arrow param's hole builds a real ERROR node and was already loud via WI-766 -- and measurement found TEN silent spellings, not three. NOTE FOR FUTURE READERS: this ticket's phrase 'Same pathology WI-440 fixed for @ {}' is accurate but easy to misread. WI-440 fixed it by making '@ {}' LEGAL (commaSep, not commaSep1, so the empty row parses), NOT by making it loud. The first implementation misread it as 'still loud' and asserted 'operation f(a: Int64) -> Int64 @ {}' is_err -- which passes VACUOUSLY, because '@ ...' is ungrammatical after an operation return type at all ('@ {Error}' and '@ Error' fail identically, 'effects {}' is clean). Caught in review and replaced with a CLEAN assertion in arrow-type position.", at: "2026-07-22T06:42:36Z")
